var OLD=[
 {t:'Read a profit and loss statement',min:8,sum:'How a company gets from the money it takes in to the profit it keeps.',key:'A profit and loss statement answers one question: over this period, did the business earn more than it spent?',
  secs:[['From sales to profit','Start at the top with <b>revenue</b>, the value of everything sold. Subtract what it cost to make those goods and you have <b>gross profit</b>. Subtract salaries, rent and marketing and you have <b>operating profit</b>. Subtract interest and tax and what remains is <b>net profit</b>, the part that belongs to shareholders.'],['Margins make companies comparable','A ₹50 crore profit means little alone. Divide profit by revenue to get a <b>margin</b>. A company keeping 20 paise of every rupee is a very different business from one keeping 5 paise, whatever their size.'],['What the statement leaves out','Sales are recorded when they are earned, not when the customer pays. So a company can report a healthy profit while its bank balance shrinks. That gap is the subject of the next two lessons.']],
  w:'pnl',ex:'Revenue                1,000\nCost of goods           −600\nGross profit             400   (40% margin)\nOperating costs         −250\nOperating profit         150   (15% margin)\nInterest                 −30\nTax at 25% of 120        −30\nNet profit                90   (9% margin)',
  cq:'Revenue doubles and every cost doubles with it. What happens to the net margin?',ca:'Nothing. Profit doubles too, so profit divided by revenue is unchanged. Margins only move when costs grow slower or faster than sales.'},
 {t:'Read a balance sheet',min:7,sum:'What a business owns, what it owes, and who funded the difference.',key:'A balance sheet is a photograph: on one day, here is everything the business owns and everything it owes.',
  secs:[['Three blocks','<b>Assets</b> are what the company owns: cash, stock, factories, money customers owe it. <b>Liabilities</b> are what it owes: loans, unpaid supplier bills. <b>Equity</b> is what is left for the owners. Assets always equal liabilities plus equity.'],['Why it always balances','Every rupee of assets was paid for by someone. Either a lender or supplier provided it, which is a liability, or the owners did, which is equity. There is no third source, so the two sides must match.'],['What analysts look for','How much of the business is funded by debt. How much cash it holds. Whether money owed by customers and unsold stock are growing faster than sales, which often signals trouble before the profit line does.']],
  ex:'Raise equity             +100\nBorrow from a bank        +50\nBuy a plant              −120\n\nAssets:      cash 30 + plant 120  = 150\nFunded by:   debt 50 + equity 100 = 150',
  cq:'A company earns ₹20 crore and pays no dividend. Which parts of the balance sheet change?',ca:'Equity rises by 20 through retained earnings. Assets rise by 20 as well, as cash if customers paid, or as money owed by customers if they have not yet.'},
 {t:'Read a cash flow statement',min:8,sum:'Where the money actually came from, and where it went.',key:'Profit can be shaped by accounting choices. Cash in the bank cannot. The cash flow statement shows which one you are looking at.',
  secs:[['Three sections','<b>Operations</b>: cash from running the business. <b>Investing</b>: cash spent on plant and equipment, or raised by selling it. <b>Financing</b>: cash from lenders and shareholders, and cash returned to them.'],['Why profit and cash differ','Selling on credit adds to profit now and to cash later. Building up stock uses cash without touching profit. Depreciation cuts profit without using any cash. The statement starts from profit and undoes each of these.'],['Free cash flow','Take cash from operations and subtract spending on plant. What remains is <b>free cash flow</b>: money the company could hand to its owners without shrinking the business. It is the number most valuations are built on.']],
  ex:'Net profit                       90\nAdd back depreciation           +40\nCustomers owe 50 more           −50\nCash from operations             80\nSpending on plant               −60\nFree cash flow                   20',
  cq:'A company has reported profits for five years, but cash from operations has been negative every year. What do you suspect?',ca:'Profit is not turning into cash. Check whether money owed by customers or unsold stock keeps growing, and whether sales are being recorded earlier than they should be.'},
 {t:'See how the three link',min:7,sum:'One event, traced through all three statements.',key:'Net profit flows into equity. The cash flow statement explains the change in cash. The balance sheet holds both.',
  secs:[['Profit lands in equity','Whatever net profit is not paid out as dividends is added to <b>retained earnings</b>, a line inside equity. That is how one year of trading changes the balance sheet.'],['Cash flow explains the cash line','Opening cash plus the total of the cash flow statement equals closing cash. If your model does not tie here, something is linked wrongly.'],['One item touches everything','Depreciation shows the links at work. It lowers profit, it is added back in the cash flow, and it reduces the value of plant. Interviewers use it for exactly that reason.']],
  ex:'Depreciation rises by 10, tax rate 25%\n\nProfit before tax        −10\nTax                     +2.5\nNet profit              −7.5\nCash: −7.5 + 10 back    +2.5\nPlant                    −10\nAssets: +2.5 − 10       −7.5  =  equity −7.5',
  cq:'A company pays a ₹30 crore dividend. Trace it through the three statements.',ca:'The profit and loss statement does not change. Cash flow shows a 30 outflow under financing. On the balance sheet, cash falls 30 and retained earnings fall 30.'},
 {t:'Ratios that matter',min:8,sum:'Turning raw numbers into comparisons across companies and years.',key:'A single number says little. A ratio says how one number stands against another, and that is what lets you compare.',
  secs:[['Profitability','Margins show profit per rupee of sales. <b>Return on equity</b>, net profit divided by equity, shows profit per rupee the owners have put in. A business earning 20% on equity can grow far faster on its own money than one earning 8%.'],['Efficiency','<b>Receivable days</b>, money owed by customers divided by revenue, times 365, shows how long customers take to pay. Rising days mean more cash locked up to produce the same sales.'],['Leverage','Debt divided by equity shows how much of the business runs on borrowed money. <b>Interest cover</b>, operating profit divided by interest, shows how easily it can pay its lenders. Read ratios as a trend over years, not as one reading.']],
  ex:'Net profit 90 ÷ equity 600           = 15% return on equity\nReceivables 150 ÷ revenue 1,000 × 365 = 55 days\nOperating profit 150 ÷ interest 30    = 5× cover',
  cq:'Return on equity rises from 15% to 22% only because the company borrowed more. Is that an improvement?',ca:'Not by itself. Debt lifts the return on equity and the risk together. Check the return on all capital, debt included, to see whether the business itself got better.'},
 {t:'Time value of money',min:9,sum:'Why a rupee today beats a rupee next year, and how compounding works.',key:'Money today can be invested, so it is worth more than the same amount later. Every valuation rests on this.',
  secs:[['Compounding','Invest at a rate <i>r</i> and after <i>n</i> years each rupee becomes (1 + r)ⁿ. The growth earns growth of its own, which is why the curve bends upward instead of rising in a straight line.'],['Discounting','Run it backwards. A rupee arriving in <i>n</i> years is worth 1 ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ today. The further away the money and the higher the rate, the less it is worth now.'],['CAGR','The <b>compound annual growth rate</b> is the one steady rate that links a starting value to an ending value: (end ÷ start) to the power of 1 ÷ years, minus 1. It smooths a bumpy path into a single comparable number.']],
  w:'cagr',ex:'₹1,00,000 at 12% for 10 years\n  1.12¹⁰ = 3.106      →  ₹3,10,585\n\n₹1,00,000 arriving in 5 years, at 10%\n  1 ÷ 1.10⁵ = 0.621   →  ₹62,092 today',
  cq:'At 12% a year, roughly how long does money take to double?',ca:'About six years. Divide 72 by the rate: 72 ÷ 12 = 6. This rule of 72 works well for rates between about 5% and 20%.'},
 {t:'Your first DCF',min:10,sum:'Valuing a business as the cash it will produce.',key:'A business is worth the cash it will hand its owners in future, discounted back to today.',
  secs:[['Forecast the cash','Estimate free cash flow for each of the next five to ten years. This is where knowledge of the business matters: how much it can sell, at what margin, and how much it must reinvest.'],['Discount it','Divide each year of cash by (1 + r)ⁿ, where <i>r</i> is the return investors require. Riskier businesses get a higher rate, so their future cash counts for less.'],['Terminal value','A business does not stop after the forecast. If cash grows at a steady rate <i>g</i> forever, everything beyond the forecast is worth next year\'s cash ÷ (r − g). This one figure is often most of the answer, so test it hard.']],
  w:'dcf',ex:'Cash next year 100, growing 5% forever, rate 10%\n  100 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05)      = 2,000\nLess net debt               −400\nValue of the shares         1,600\n÷ 80 crore shares         = ₹200 a share',
  cq:'In that example, the discount rate moves from 10% to 11%. What happens to the value of the business?',ca:'It falls to 100 ÷ 0.06 = 1,667, a drop of about 17%. A one-point change in one input moved the answer by a sixth, which is why a DCF gives a range, not a number.'},
 {t:'Multiples and when they mislead',min:8,sum:'The shortcut everyone uses, and the traps inside it.',key:'A multiple is a DCF folded into one number. Growth, risk and returns are all still in there; you just cannot see them.',
  secs:[['The two you will meet first','<b>P/E</b> is the share price divided by earnings per share. <b>EV/EBITDA</b> is the value of the whole business, debt included, divided by operating profit before depreciation. The second lets you compare companies with different amounts of debt.'],['Why two companies deserve different multiples','Faster growth, higher returns on capital and lower risk all justify paying more per rupee of profit. A stock at 30 times earnings can be cheaper than one at 10 if it turns far more of its profit into cash for owners.'],['Three traps','Cyclical companies look cheapest at the peak, just before profit falls. Companies with heavy debt look cheap on P/E. And profit itself depends on accounting choices, so check what is inside it.']],
  ex:'Both companies: P/E 20, growth 8%\n\nA earns 25% on equity → reinvests 8 ÷ 25 = 32%, pays out 68%\nB earns 10% on equity → reinvests 8 ÷ 10 = 80%, pays out 20%\n\nSame price per rupee of profit. A hands over 3.4× the cash.',
  cq:'A steel maker trades at 5 times earnings when steel prices are at a record high. Is it cheap?',ca:'Probably not. Its profit is at a peak that will not last. On profit averaged across a full cycle, the multiple could be 12 or 15. For cyclical companies, a low P/E at the top is a warning.'},
 {t:'Build a one-page model',min:10,sum:'Linking a few assumptions so that changing one moves everything after it.',key:'A model is a chain of linked assumptions. Its value is in showing what must be true for a number to hold.',
  secs:[['Start from drivers','Do not type in revenue. Build it from what causes it: tonnes sold times price, stores times sales per store, customers times spend. Then every forecast is a claim about the real world that you can check.'],['Link the statements','Profit feeds equity. Depreciation and spending on plant move the asset base. The cash flow statement produces closing cash. Once linked, one change runs through all three statements by itself.'],['Add checks','The balance sheet must balance. Closing cash must match. And every driver must stay physically possible: a plant cannot sell more than it can make. Models rarely fail on arithmetic. They fail on assumptions nobody tested.']],
  ex:'Volume        10 million tonnes\nPrice         ₹5,000 a tonne     → revenue ₹5,000 crore\nProfit        ₹1,000 a tonne     → operating profit ₹1,000 crore\n\nCheck: capacity 12 mt × 90% = 10.8 mt.  Volume fits.',
  cq:'Your model has the company selling 14 million tonnes in year four. Capacity is 12 million and no new plant is planned. What do you do?',ca:'Treat it as an error. Either cap volume at what the plants can make, around 10.8 million tonnes at 90% use, or add a new plant with its cost and build time. A forecast that breaks a physical limit is wrong however neat the spreadsheet.'}
];

var GLOSS=[['EBITDA','Profit before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation; a rough measure of operating cash profit.'],['Enterprise value (EV)','What it costs to buy the whole business: the value of the shares plus net debt.'],['Free cash flow','Cash from operations minus spending on plant and equipment.'],['P/E ratio','Share price divided by earnings per share.'],['DCF','Valuing a business as the sum of its future cash flows, each discounted back to today.'],['Terminal value','The part of a DCF that covers every year after the forecast ends.'],['Cost of capital (WACC)','The blended return lenders and shareholders require.'],['Return on equity','Net profit divided by shareholders\' funds.'],['Working capital','Receivables plus inventory minus payables; cash tied up in day-to-day trading.'],['Receivable days','How long customers take to pay, on average.'],['Consensus','The average of brokers\' published forecasts for a company.'],['Initiation','The first full report an analyst publishes on a company.'],['Variant view','Where your forecast differs from consensus, and why.'],['Reverse DCF','Working back from the share price to the growth it assumes.'],['Channel check','Asking dealers, distributors or customers what is actually selling.'],['Duration','How much a bond\'s price moves when its yield moves.'],['Interest cover','Operating profit divided by interest cost.'],['Basis point','One hundredth of a percentage point.']];


var MEMOS=[
 ['Why EBITDA flatters heavy industries','EBITDA ignores the cost of replacing worn-out plant. For a software firm that hardly matters. For a cement or telecom company, replacement spending can eat half of it. Two companies on the same EV/EBITDA can differ widely on the cash left for shareholders. For asset-heavy sectors, compare on EBIT or free cash flow as well.'],
 ['Read the cash flow statement backwards','Start at the bottom: did cash go up or down? Then ask where it came from. Borrowing, selling shares and selling assets are all different from earning it. Only then look at operations, and compare operating cash with reported profit over five years. A persistent gap is the first thing to explain.'],
 ['What "priced in" means','A share price is a forecast. If a stock trades at 40 times earnings, buyers expect profit to grow for many years. A reverse DCF turns the price into that growth rate. The better step is to turn the growth into physical terms, such as tonnes, stores or customers, and ask whether the business could really deliver them.']
];


